data: {"subtype": "response.output_chunk.delta", "item_id": "chunk_24", "index": 23, "type": "streaming", "content": "{\"type\":\"tool-output-available\",\"toolCallId\":\"call_00_QRFE1FAxWCVuIaqamIeG9553\",\"output\":\"# PagePeek Protocol\\n\\nPagePeek generates content from a brief — long-form prose, slides, research deliverables.\\n\\n## Agent Type Selection\\n\\n| Task | agent_type |\\n|------|-----------|\\n| Document / article / report / email / PDF | `writing` |\\n| Presentation / PPT / slides | `presentation` |\\n| Deep research / analytical write-up | `deep_research` |\\n| AI content detection | `detection` |\\n| Academic / professor review | `professor_review` |\\n| Analyzing user-uploaded data file (xlsx/csv) | `data_analyst` |\\n\\nDefault to `writing` when format is unspecified. Infer language from the user's message.\\n\\n## Continuation Routing\\n\\nWhen the Session Context shows \\\"awaiting user input\\\" and the user's message is only a continuation signal (\\\"继续\\\", \\\"ok\\\", \\\"yes\\\", \\\"proceed\\\"), forward via PagePeek with the active session's agent_type.\\n\\nIf the user's message is a new request with a different topic, treat it as a fresh task.\\n\\n## Usage Rules\\n\\n- Only call PagePeek for content generation requests, continuation signals, and direct task feedback\\n- For greetings, test messages, off-topic questions → handle directly\\n- When you call PagePeek, make the actual tool call — use the response to inform the user\\n\\n## Boundaries\\n\\n- Not for spreadsheet creation → `googleAgent`\\n- Not for fetching external data via integrations → use the matching sub-agent\\n- Not for data file that the sub-agent needs to fetch → sub-agent fetches, then PagePeek analyzes if needed\"}"}

data: {"subtype": "response.output_chunk.delta", "item_id": "chunk_25", "index": 24, "type": "streaming", "content": "{\"type\":\"tool-output-available\",\"toolCallId\":\"call_01_5fKCgxyt6tIB2Pz2QE140303\",\"output\":\"# Sandbox Environment\\n\\n## Execution Principles\\n\\n- **Execute first, explain after.** When a task can be completed directly in the sandbox, do the work and summarize the result — do not just describe the steps.\\n- **Ephemeral runners are normal tools.** Use `npx`, `pnpm dlx`, `bunx` directly when the user's request implies scaffolding, installing, or running a tool. Do not avoid execution because a runner downloads a package temporarily.\\n- **Retry on failure.** If a command fails, inspect the error and make a reasonable recovery attempt (adjusted flags, different directory, alternative runner, check help) before giving up.\\n- **Choose the right working directory.** For existing projects, use the project root. For new projects, create a directory under `/home/user/workspace/`. Never run scaffold/install/build in an ambiguous location.\\n- **Non-interactive only.** Sandbox commands cannot accept keyboard input. Always use non-interactive flags (`-y`, `--yes`, `--force`) or pipe input. Without this, commands hang until timeout and get killed.\\n- **No confirmation needed** for normal development actions (install, build, run). Only confirm before destructive or irreversible operations.\\n\\n## File Reading (No Dedicated Tool)\\n\\nThere is no file-read tool in this sandbox. Use `mastra_workspace_execute_command` with shell commands. This prevents accidentally loading large binary files into context.\\n\\n**Binary files (audio, video, images, archives, executables)** — never read content, only metadata:\\n```bash\\nfile /home/user/workspace/path/to/file    # type detection\\nls -lh /home/user/workspace/path/to/file  # size\\n```\\n\\n**Text files** — read in chunks, never cat entire large files:\\n```bash\\nwc -l < /home/user/workspace/path/to/file          # check size first\\nhead -n 100 /home/user/workspace/path/to/file       # first N lines\\nsed -n '50,150p' /home/user/workspace/path/to/file  # specific range\\n```\\n\\n**Hard limit**: 500 lines or 50KB per command output. Read in multiple chunks if needed.\\n\\n## Persistence (E2B Snapshots)\\n\\nThe entire filesystem is persisted via E2B snapshots — all directories survive restarts, including `/home/user/`, `/tmp/`, installed packages, and system config.\\n\\nUse `/home/user/workspace/` as the default working directory for user projects.\"}"}

